Everybody was bowing to them—cabmen, little children, and everybody
      else—and they were returning all the bows and overlooking nobody,
      when a young lady met them and made a deep courtesy.

“Good stranger, I have no friends, all are at rest these many years;
      ye speak me fair, ye mean me well, but I know ye not; I am alone and
      forlorn in the world—prithee lead me to her grave.”

“Well, I don’t know. I didn’t know I was doing any harm;
      I didn’t <i>mean</i> to do any harm. They were very nice people, and
      they seemed to like me.”

